00:48:45,517 --> 00:48:51,517
Ravenous is a 1999 horror Western film directed by Antonia Bird and starring
Guy Pearce, Robert Carlyle, Jeffrey Jones, and David Arquette.

15
00:52:14,482 --> 00:52:20,482
Set in the 1840s in California's Sierra Nevada, the film follows a group of
soldiers at a military outpost who encounter a sadistic cannibal.

16
00:55:43,448 --> 00:55:49,448
Screenwriter Ted Griffin's screenplay combines elements from the Donner Party and that of Alfred Packer, the real-life "Colorado
Cannibal" who survived by eating five companions after becoming snowbound in the San Juan Mountains in the 1870s.

17
00:59:12,413 --> 00:59:18,413
However, the film's plot also serves as an overt criticism of
manifest destiny through its use of cannibalism.

18
01:02:41,379 --> 01:02:47,379
By turning the act into an insatiable hunger, the voracious need to eat human flesh is equated to the all-consuming pursuit of power and
wealth that was inherent to the expansionist attitudes of those seeking to settle the American frontier in the 19th century.

19
01:06:10,344 --> 01:06:16,344
The film would be the last theatrical release to feature John Spencer.

20
01:09:39,310 --> 01:09:45,310
An international co-production between United Kingdom, United States
and Mexico, Ravenous had a troubled production history.

21
01:13:08,275 --> 01:13:14,275
Issues over budget and shooting schedules were still ongoing
when filming was about to start in Slovakia.

22
01:16:37,241 --> 01:16:43,241
After the original director Milcho Manchevski was fired three weeks into production, he was replaced by Bird at the
suggestion of actor Robert Carlyle who had previously worked with her in 1997 in the movie Face.

23
01:20:06,206 --> 01:20:12,206
The film was shot in Europe and Mexico.

24
01:23:35,172 --> 01:23:41,172
Michael Nyman and Damon Albarn composed the film's score, which generated a significant amount of
interest for its quirky and inventive use of loops, instruments and musical structure.

25
01:27:04,137 --> 01:27:10,137
Released by 20th Century Fox in the spring of 1999, Ravenous was a
box-office bomb, grossing $2 million against a $12 million budget.

26
01:30:33,103 --> 01:30:39,103
However, despite initial reception being mixed when released, it
has since garnered a reputation as a cult film.

27
